      "text": "where m(u,v) = (u2 + v2 + 1)−21(n−1). We can omit the term m(u,v) by a time rescaling of p(X). Therefore, we obtain a polynomial expression of p(X) in each Ui. The expressions of p(X) in each Vi is the same as that for each Ui, except by a multiplicative factor of (−1)n−1. In these coordinates for i ∈ {1,2}, v = 0 always represents the points of S1 and thus the inﬁnity of R2. Note that S1 is invariant under the ﬂow of p(X).",
      "html": null,
      "level": null,
      "section_hierarchy": []
    },
    {
      "block_type": "text",
      "box": {
        "page": 0,
        "x0": 0.21646082002767414,
        "y0": 0.29885929625632046,
        "x1": 0.6700149502431628,
        "y1": 0.315770948286786
      },
      "text": "Q(x1,...,xm) = (x1,...,xr,−xr+1,...,−xm). The result now follows from the fact that ϕ is Ck-conjugated to Q by σ.",
      "html": null,
      "level": null,
      "section_hierarchy": []
    },
    {
      "block_type": "text",
      "box": {
        "page": 0,
        "x0": 0.2966729118771795,
        "y0": 0.6728859939883463,
        "x1": 0.8589017274797833,
        "y1": 0.7757668140700885
      },
      "text": "where m = i, n = i and m < n. Denoting by (u,v) the image of φi and ψi in every chart (therefore (u,v) play diﬀerent roles in each chart) one can see the following expressions for p(X):",
      "html": null,
      "level": null,
      "section_hierarchy": []
    },
    {
      "block_type": "text",
      "box": {
        "page": 0,
        "x0": 0.2260879720472464,
        "y0": 0.10290495304698019,
        "x1": 0.8156081177678405,
        "y1": 0.24710004947092054
      },
      "text": "Therefore σ conjugates ϕ and Q. In particular it follows that Fix(Q) ⊂ Rm is sub vector space of dimension r. From (6) we know that Q is an involution. Thus from statements (i) and (ii) we have that there is a linear change of variables such that in these new variables Q is given by,",
      "html": null,
